          "location": "Section 4, Figure 4a and 4b",
          "explanation": "The paper presents a strong, systematic correlation: knowledge entropy (Figure 2) and knowledge acquisition/retention (Figure 4a) decrease monotonically across pretraining steps. The hypothesis that low entropy (fewer active memory vectors) leads to frequent overwriting of a small set of active vectors, hindering new knowledge integration, is explicitly stated in Sections 4 and 4.3 and motivated by the chain rule analysis in Appendix A.1. While a perfect intervention proving causality is challenging, the resuscitation experiment (Section 4.3, Figure 5) serves as a direct *manipulation* test. Artificially increasing the activity of low-coefficient vectors in a final-stage model improves its knowledge acquisition and retention. This result, where modifying the proposed mechanism (coefficient distribution) leads to the predicted change in outcome (performance), provides evidence beyond mere correlation."
        },
        {
          "concern": "Experimental scope is limited to OLMo models only.",
          "location": "Section 3.2, Appendix A.2, Figure 6",
          "explanation": "The paper acknowledges the focus on OLMo, justified by it being one of the few models providing extensive intermediate checkpoints (Section 6, Limitations). To address generalizability, we validated the core knowledge entropy trend on the Pythia 1.4B model (Appendix A.2, Figure 6), showing the same decreasing trend. Furthermore, we demonstrate the knowledge acquisition/forgetting trend holds across varied hyperparameters (Section 4.2, Appendix B.4) and two model sizes (1B and 7B) within OLMo. The consistency across these dimensions strengthens the observed phenomenon."
        },
        {
          "concern": "The resuscitation method appears arbitrary.",
          "location": "Section 4.3, Algorithm 1",
          "explanation": "We agree the method is a targeted intervention designed explicitly to test the hypothesis, not a proposed production-ready solution. Its purpose is diagnostic. The algorithm scales parameters in the up-projection matrix `K` associated with the lowest `p%` of average coefficients `C`. This directly increases the contribution of previously inactive memory vectors during forward passes, effectively *increasing knowledge entropy*. The observation that this leads to improved acquisition/retention (Figure 5) supports the causal link that low entropy hinders performance. The paper explicitly notes this is insufficient to fully restore plasticity and that more fundamental approaches are needed (Section 4.3, final paragraph)."
        }
      ],
      "proposed_future_revisions": [
        {
          "concern": "Strengthen the discussion of causality and the role of the resuscitation experiment.",
          "proposed_change": "We will revise the text in Sections 4.3 and 5 to more explicitly frame the resuscitation experiment as a causal manipulation test. We will clarify that while perfect causal isolation in complex systems like LLMs is difficult, the intervention provides strong supporting evidence by showing that manipulating the proposed mechanism (coefficient distribution/entropy) produces the predicted change in model behavior (acquisition/forgetting).",
          "requires_new_experiment_or_analysis": false
        },
        {
          "concern": "Acknowledge the limitation of model scope more prominently and discuss steps for future generalization.",
          "proposed_change": "We will expand the Limitations section (Section 6) to more prominently discuss the need to validate findings on other model families (e.g., Llama, Gemma) as a key direction for future work, pending the release of intermediate checkpoints.",
          "requires_new_experiment_or_analysis": false
        },
        {
          "concern": "Clarify that the resuscitation method is a proof-of-concept intervention, not a proposed solution.",
          "proposed_change": "We will revise the description in Section 4.3 to more clearly state that Algorithm 1 is an *intervention designed to test the hypothesis* about the role of inactive memory vectors. We will reiterate in the conclusion that it highlights a direction for future work (developing more refined methods to modulate entropy) rather than presenting a final solution.",
          "requires_new_experiment_or_analysis": false
        }
      ],
      "response": "Thank you for your thoughtful review and for acknowledging the novelty of the knowledge entropy concept. We appreciate the opportunity to clarify our contributions regarding causality, generalizability, and the purpose of our interventions.\n\nFirst, regarding **causality**: We agree that establishing perfect causality in complex systems is challenging. Our work progresses from correlation (Section 4.2) to a targeted intervention (Section 4.3). The core hypothesis is that low knowledge entropy (concentrated updates on few memory vectors) impedes new knowledge integration. The resuscitation experiment directly tests this by artificially broadening the coefficient distribution in late-stage models—effectively increasing their knowledge entropy. The subsequent improvement in acquisition and retention (Figure 5) provides strong evidence that the entropy mechanism is functionally relevant, moving beyond mere observation. We will clarify this causal framing in the revision.\n\nSecond, on **generalizability**: We focused on OLMo due to its unique provision of public intermediate checkpoints, essential for our longitudinal study. We validated the central entropy trend on Pythia (Appendix A.2, Figure 6) and showed consistent results across model sizes (1B/7B), datasets, and hyperparameters. We acknowledge that broader validation across architectures is an important future step, which we will emphasize more strongly in the Limitations section.\n\nFinally, concerning the **resuscitation method**: We designed it as a diagnostic tool to test the hypothesis, not as an optimized solution. Its simplicity is a feature for clear interpretation. The result confirms that manipulating the coefficient distribution alters continual learning performance, supporting our central claim. We explicitly note its limitations and the need for more sophisticated methods in Section 4.3. We will revise the text to make this proof-of-concept intent clearer.\n\nOverall, our primary contribution is identifying and providing mechanistic evidence for a previously unexplored property of LLMs—the decay of integrative capacity during pretraining—which has direct implications for strategies involving further training of pretrained models. We believe these insights are significant for the community."
    }
  ]
}
