            "affected_claim": "The central claim of a well-defined, theoretically grounded CCOP formulation for temporally sparse attacks on LLM-based forecasters.",
            "fixable_without_substantial_new_work": true
        },
        {
            "weakness": "The claimed linear complexity O(T × τ) for Algorithm 1 is unsubstantiated and appears incorrect given the nested loops and independent loss computations for each candidate time step, suggesting a much higher query complexity that is not analyzed.",
            "severity": "major",
            "evidence": "Algorithm 1 description states 'the computation complexity of the proposed method is O (T × τ ), whereas a standard greedy algorithm has a significantly higher complexity of O (T^τ).' This claim is not derived or justified. The algorithm involves selecting τ indices from T candidates, computing a loss for each candidate j (requiring a model query per candidate per iteration), and repeating until convergence. This suggests a complexity scaling with the number of queries (T per iteration) and iterations, not simply T*τ. The query budget, a critical metric for black-box attacks, is never reported.",
            "affected_claim": "The claim of an efficient, practical black-box attack method suitable for real-world constrained scenarios.",
            "fixable_without_substantial_new_work": true
        }
    ],
