      "proposed_future_revisions": [
        {
          "concern": "Resolve the mathematical inconsistency between additive and multiplicative perturbation frameworks.",
          "proposed_change": "We will revise the manuscript to eliminate all ambiguity. We will: 1) Explicitly define the additive perturbation ρ_i = w_i · X_i immediately after Eq. (3) to formally bridge the multiplicative variable w and the additive change ρ. 2) Reformulate Eq. (5) using the multiplicative variable w_i for consistency: max_{w_i} L(f(Xt ◦ (1 + e_i · w_i)), Ŷt) s.t. |w_i| ≤ ε, where ◦ denotes element-wise multiplication and e_i is the standard basis vector. 3) Ensure Algorithm 1's description and all subsequent text consistently use the multiplicative variable w.",
          "requires_new_experiment_or_analysis": false
        },
        {
          "concern": "Provide a correct complexity analysis and report query counts.",
          "proposed_change": "We will retract the claim of O(T × τ) complexity. We will instead provide a detailed analysis of the query complexity. Each iteration of Algorithm 1 requires O(T) queries to compute losses for candidate indices (line 4) and O(τ) queries to refine the support set (line 7). If the algorithm converges in K iterations, the total query complexity is O(K · (T + τ)). We will report the average number of queries (K · (T + τ)) used in our experiments in a revised version of Table 1 or in a new table, as this is a critical metric for black-box attacks.",
          "requires_new_experiment_or_analysis": true
        },
        {
          "concern": "Strengthen the experimental evaluation with comparisons to established sparse attack baselines.",
          "proposed_change": "We will conduct new experiments comparing TSA against two strong baselines: 1) A White-box L0-PGD attack (Projected Gradient Descent with an L0 constraint) on a surrogate model, representing a strong but less practical upper bound. 2) A Black-box Greedy Coordinate Ascent (GCA) method, which iteratively selects the best time step to perturb. This will provide a more rigorous benchmark for TSA's performance and efficiency.",
          "requires_new_experiment_or_analysis": true
        }
      ],
      "response": "We thank the reviewer for their rigorous assessment and for holding our work to a high standard. We fully acknowledge that the current submission has soundness and evaluation shortcomings that prevent acceptance. Our goal in this rebuttal is not to argue for acceptance of the current version but to demonstrate a clear and feasible path to comprehensively address these concerns in a major revision.\n\n**On Mathematical Soundness:** The reviewer is correct. The current manuscript contains a presentation flaw—an inconsistent switch between additive and multiplicative notations—that obscures an otherwise sound underlying model. The core formulation in Eq. (3) uses a multiplicative variable **w** to naturally enforce the per-time-step magnitude constraint derived in Eq. (4). The operations in Eqs. (5), (6), and Algorithm 1 are computations on this variable **w**. The additive form in Eq. (5) (Xt + {0,..., wi·Xi,...,0}) is mathematically equivalent to the multiplicative form (Xt ◦ (1 + e_i · w_i)) because wi·Xi is the additive perturbation ρ_i. However, we failed to explicitly state this equivalence, causing confusion. We will revise the paper to use a consistent, rigorous mathematical presentation throughout, as detailed in our proposed changes.\n\n**On Complexity and Query Analysis:** We concede entirely. The claim of O(T × τ) complexity was an error. The computational cost is dominated by the number of queries to the black-box model. A correct analysis shows the complexity is O(K · (T + τ)) queries, where K is the number of iterations. We will remove the incorrect claim, provide the proper analysis, and—crucially—report the actual query counts from our experiments. This data is available and will be included to properly evaluate the attack's efficiency, a key aspect of its practicality.\n\n**On Experimental Evaluation:** We agree that the comparison to Gaussian White Noise alone is insufficient to demonstrate superiority over other adversarial strategies. To properly validate our method's contribution, we will conduct new experiments comparing TSA against a white-box L0-PGD baseline (on a surrogate model) and a black-box Greedy Coordinate Ascent baseline. This will provide a much stronger foundation for evaluating TSA's effectiveness and efficiency.\n\nIn summary, we recognize that the current submission does not meet the required standard due to these issues. We are committed to performing the substantial revisions outlined above—correcting the mathematical presentation, providing a sound complexity analysis with query counts, and strengthening the experimental comparisons—to create a version of this work that is technically sound, rigorously evaluated, and suitable for a top-tier venue."
